    "method": "Final LLVM baseline/candidate measured sequentially, five fresh repetitions, single worker, no cache; all sources feed independent checksum checks in sync/async guests. Native Fast timings compare only the two native builds and never to LLVM.",
    "held_variants": "Gate removal alone improved compilation but grew distinct 128 output from 30528 to 118542B. Input tokens were marked mutable as if the helper owned them, forcing 128 token stores/loads per call. Excluding the borrowed input prefix restored all descriptor aliases; omitting unread direct result slots then removed the narrow-case overhead.",
    "source_limit": "Intermediate native variants were exploratory rather than source-frozen. Raw compiler and generated-workload hashes survive in their reports; the final candidate is source-frozen above.",
    "final_limits": "At width128, distinct-owner output grows 620B (2.03%) while shared-owner output shrinks 12B. Width32 shared/distinct output shrinks 1997/2686B. Do not extrapolate these ratios to the whole game."
  },
  "contracts": {
    "admission": "Result and ordered event shapes retain only input owner ordinals. Actual caller depths, source/type witnesses, read-before-write order and alias topology remain checked. Fresh owner results/event escape fall back or reject. Input freeze/reserve/pop/truncate retain barriers.",
    "tokens": "Only closeRuntime/release rewrite root token locals; helper cleanup ranges start after all input roots. Exempt only that compiled child prefix, preserving local/export roots plus independent loop/demand marks.",
    "result_slots": "A complete nonempty alias recipe has zero result stores/loads. Direct caller omits slot/mark/rewind, keeps logical W+1 mask, and supplies an unread default-zero pointer to unchanged signatures. Partial results and packed packets retain allocation. Calls, arguments and owner events still execute."
  },
